  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry conditions through midweek, with rain \nchances late week.\n\nA larger amplitude upper level shortwave trough is currently\nprogressing across the Great Lakes region today. A cold front in\nassociation with this upper level trough is currently draped\nacross central Indiana and northern Ohio, and is progged to push\nthrough the region during the overnight hours. Given the timing\nof the passage of the front, much of the area will remain dry,\nwith only light showers possible for upslope locations across\nportions of SE West Virginia and SW Virginia. In the wake of\nthis frontal passage, surface high pressure looks to settle\nacross PA and NY, which will lead to drying across much of the\narea. While temperatures look to continue to remain elevated in\nthe low to mid 80s across the mountains, and mid to upper 80s in\nthe Piedmont, dewpoint temperatures look to drop into the mid\n50s to low 60s across the area, which will make for much more\ncomfortable weather across the region through the middle of this\nweek. \n\nTemperatures remain elevated areawide primarily because upper\nlevel ridging looks to build in late Monday into the region as\nthe aforementioned upper level shortwave ejects east of the \narea. Overall, the area looks to remain dry for much of the \nweek, with a gradual return of surface dewpoints back into the \nlow to mid 60s by Friday. Embedded upper level shortwave \ndisturbances rounding the top of the upper level trough looks to\nbring low rain chances back into the forecast by Thursday for \nprimarily mountain locations. The aforementioned upper level \nridge looks to possibly break down by next weekend, with no \nsignificant rain chances expected prior to this.",
